   "author": "Hollowmere team",
   "story": "The Savings & Loan was where the Cordon paid Pell's Crossing to leave: a hundred dollars a head, cash or scrip, signed for at the teller line on presentation of a screening card. On the last day the line ran down Larch Street to the river. Manager Ruth Calloway bolted the front doors at three o'clock sharp with the line still outside, and the line came in through the staff-room window. Ruth, her head teller Dale Ostrom and the Cordon paymaster, Sergeant Vance, shut themselves in the vault with the stipend cash. Ruth had set the time lock to manual so they could let themselves out in the morning. Vance had a bite on his hand. He said it was a dog.",
